kind: perf
upstream: not for xatlas (whose last code change was in 2022): the hooks are
trimkit's, so xatlas's task groups run on the extension's one pool.

xatlas started a thread per core for every atlas (`TaskScheduler`'s workers,
a mutex and condition variable each) and ran its task groups on them: the
meshes, each mesh's chart groups, and each group's charts, nested. The build
defines XA_MULTITHREADED=0, so none of that is compiled, and this changes the
serial scheduler left in its place so `wait` hands a group's tasks to
`TrimkitRunTasks` (xatlas.h), which src/xatlas.cpp points at the pool
(src/parallel.hpp through common.hpp's `run_ranges`). xatlas keeps scratch per
thread (`ThreadLocal`, indexed by `currentThreadIndex`), so each thread
running tasks has the pool's index for it, below `TrimkitThreadSlots()` (the
CPUs the machine has online), which `ThreadLocal` is sized by: no two threads
running one call's tasks share one. Tasks create groups on any thread, so the
serial scheduler's growing table becomes a fixed array of slots claimed by
compare-and-swap, with no lock: one atlas has one caller, at most
`TrimkitThreadSlots()` threads run its tasks, each inside at most three
nested groups, so 4 a thread and 4 more can't run out (and if they did it
would be a broken promise, which stops the process with that said, never a
wrong answer).

Why: one pool for the whole extension, whose workers exit when idle, and no
threads started per call; measured 8-21% faster for `parametrize`, the same
answers to the byte. It also retires xatlas-lost-wakeup.patch, a fix to the
worker loop that is no longer compiled. An exception a task throws now comes
back to the calling thread rather than ending the process; xatlas allocates
through libc, so in practice that is the pool's own (a fork during the call,
or the interpreter's exit).

Covered by: tests/test_xatlas.py (the answers), tests/test_threads.py
(parametrize on 8 threads gives the same bytes as one, a forked child and an
exiting interpreter), and fuzz/xatlas.cpp, which runs xatlas on the real pool
under AddressSanitizer.

